README.OSK -- agrep 2.01 on OS-9/68000ORIG/ is agrep 2.01 as the Microware OS-9 archive carried it(OSK_SRC_TO_PORT/agrep.lzh): Sun Wu and Udi Manber's approximate grep fromthe University of Arizona, the same release comp.sources.unix posted involume 26, with no OS-9 changes.  copyright and agrep.1 are kept beside theport as well.The program itself is unchanged.  Four things kept it from working, and eachis under #ifdef OSK:  BUFFERS.  agrep sizes its buffers for a Unix machine: 49,152-byte blocks,    a 66,000-entry automaton and a 260,000-byte pattern file, over a megabyte    of static data where l68 stops at 64K, and locals of up to 147K where a    68000 stack displacement reaches 32K.  Microware C's `remote' storage    class lifts both limits.  File-scope buffers are declared REMOTE and the    big local ones LREMOTE (static remote -- none of those functions calls    itself).  asearch.c, asearch1.c, bitap.c, main.c, mgrep.c and sgrep.c    define the pair at the top.  MEASURED 2026-09-15: a 300,000-byte remote    array and a 200,000-byte static remote local both link and read back.  OPENING FILES.  main.c opens with mode 0, Unix's read-only.  On OS-9 mode    0 grants no access: the open succeeds and every read fails, so a file    named on the command line was searched as empty.  A macro in main.c turns    mode 0 into 1, read.  PUTCHAR.  <stdio.h>'s putchar() is a macro that uses its argument twice,    so main.c's and mgrep.c's putchar(*p++) printed every other character of    a matched record.  In those two files putchar() is fputc().  ENOENT.  checkfile.c tests errno against ENOENT, which OS-9 names E_PNNF.